data.content:
Here are the three breaking changes in EF Core 10 that affect your code and how to fix each:

---

## 1. `ExecuteUpdateAsync` — Now accepts a regular lambda (not an expression tree)

Your manual expression tree building **will no longer compile** because the parameter type changed from `Expression<Func<...>>` to `Func<...>`.

**Fix** — replace the entire block with:

```csharp
await context.Blogs.ExecuteUpdateAsync(s =>
{
    s.SetProperty(b => b.Views, 8);
    if (nameChanged)
    {
        s.SetProperty(b => b.Name, "foo");
    }
});
```

No more expression trees needed.

---

## 2. Complex type column names are now uniquified / nested paths changed

Two sub-changes:
- **Same-named properties** across complex types (e.g. `ShippingAddress.Street` and `BillingAddress.Street`) now get a disambiguating suffix instead of silently sharing a column.
- **Nested complex types** now use the full path (`Payment_BillingAddress_City` instead of `BillingAddress_City`).

**Fix** — explicitly pin the old column names:

```csharp
modelBuilder.Entity<Customer>(b =>
{
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.ShippingAddress, p =>
        p.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("Street"));
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.BillingAddress, p =>
        p.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("Street"));
});

// For nested complex types, pin old names:
modelBuilder.Entity<Order>()
    .ComplexProperty(o => o.Payment)
    .ComplexProperty(p => p.BillingAddress)
    .Property(a => a.City)
    .HasColumnName("BillingAddress_City");
```

Or generate a new migration to adopt the new column names (preferred for greenfield).

---

## 3. `IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger` — new `logCommandText` parameter

All methods like `CommandReaderExecuting` now require an additional `string logCommandText` parameter (contains redacted SQL for logging).

**Fix:**

```csharp
public class MyLogger : IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger
{
    public InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> CommandReaderExecuting(
        IRelationalConnection connection,
        DbCommand command,
        DbContext context,
        Guid commandId,
        Guid connectionId,
        DateTimeOffset startTime,
        string logCommandText)   // ← add this parameter
    {
        // Use logCommandText for logging (sensitive data redacted)
        // Use command.CommandText for actual execution SQL
    }
}
```

---

**Summary:** The biggest win is #1 — you can delete all that expression-tree plumbing and use a simple imperative lambda. For #2, either pin column names or re-scaffold your migration. For #3, add the new `string logCommandText` parameter to every method in the interface.
